Scope of the MIT grant
----------------------

The MIT grant above covers code and documentation created specifically for
chicCanva. It does not relicense third-party libraries, artwork, fonts, model
data, services, or other material identified below. Each third-party component
remains governed by its own license and terms. A distributed chicCanva HTML
bundle contains some of these components, including AGPL-covered code, so the
bundle as distributed must also satisfy their applicable conditions.

Third-party software and content
--------------------------------

1. Fabric.js 5.1.0
   Purpose: canvas object model, rendering, selection, grouping, and transforms.
   License: MIT.
   Copyright: 2008-2015 Printio (Juriy Zaytsev, Maxim Chernyak);
              2016-present Andrea Bogazzi, Shachar Nen, and contributors.
   Source and license: https://github.com/fabricjs/fabric.js

2. jsPDF
   Purpose: client-side PDF generation.
   License: MIT.
   Copyright: 2010-2025 James Hall; 2015-2025 yWorks GmbH and contributors.
   Source and license: https://github.com/parallax/jsPDF

3. PDF.js 3.11.174
   Purpose: local parsing and rasterization of imported PDF pages.
   License: Apache License 2.0.
   Copyright: Mozilla Foundation and PDF.js contributors.
   Source: https://github.com/mozilla/pdf.js
   Bundled license copy: development/vendor/pdfjs-LICENSE.txt

4. ONNX Runtime Web
   Purpose: local browser inference for background removal.
   License: MIT, with additional upstream third-party notices applicable to
            bundled dependencies.
   Source and notices: https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime

5. @imgly/background-removal 1.7.0
   Purpose: local foreground segmentation/background removal.
   License: GNU Affero General Public License version 3 (AGPL-3.0).
   Copyright: IMG.LY GmbH and contributors.
   Source and license: https://github.com/imgly/background-removal-js

   This component is not relicensed under MIT. Its code is embedded in the
   generated chicCanva HTML. Distribution or network use of a modified build
   must comply with the AGPL, including applicable source-availability and
   notice requirements. IMG.LY may offer separate licensing terms.

6. OpenMoji 17 graphics and metadata
   Purpose: emoji browsing, smart emoji conversion, and SVG assets.
   Graphics license: Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
                     (CC BY-SA 4.0).
   Upstream code license: LGPL-3.0 where upstream code is used.
   Project: HfG Schwäbisch Gmünd, Benedikt Groß, Daniel Utz, students, and
            OpenMoji contributors.
   Source and license: https://github.com/hfg-gmuend/openmoji

   Required attribution:
   "All emojis designed by OpenMoji – the open-source emoji and icon project.
   License: CC BY-SA 4.0."

   chicCanva can recolor and change the stroke width of OpenMoji black/outline
   artwork. Such output is adapted OpenMoji material and must retain attribution,
   identify the modification where appropriate, and be shared under CC BY-SA
   4.0 or a compatible license as required by that license.

7. Font files loaded through Google Fonts or Fontsource-compatible CDNs
   Purpose: user-selected typefaces.
   License: each font retains its own upstream license. Many are distributed
            under the SIL Open Font License, Apache License 2.0, or Ubuntu Font
            License, but there is no single license for the complete catalog.
   chicCanva's original catalog categorization does not relicense font binaries.

8. Openclipart artwork
   Purpose: user-selected external clipart.
   Openclipart represents its published artwork as public-domain/CC0 material.
   Individual source/provenance should be retained and checked where required.
   Site: https://openclipart.org/

Remote services
---------------

Puter (whose upstream main repository is AGPL-3.0-only), selected model providers reached through Puter, MyMemory translation,
Google Fonts, Fontsource CDNs, jsDelivr, Openclipart, and staticimgly.com are
remote services or distribution endpoints. They are not granted under the
chicCanva MIT license. Their current terms, privacy policies, quotas, and content
licenses apply when a user enables or invokes the corresponding feature.

